    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p26-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why she is correct.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "A mode, when one exists, is a most frequent data value, so it belongs to the dataset. A mean need not: $0$ and $10$ have mean $5$.",
          "worked": "A mode is a value occurring most frequently in the dataset, so any mode must be a data value. For the data $0,10$:\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\bar{x}&=\\frac{0+10}{2}\\\\\\bar{x}&=5\\end{aligned}$$\nSince $5$ is absent from the data, the mean need not be a data value. This example demonstrates the mean claim; it has no mode under the booklet’s convention.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Does the median always have to be a part of the dataset? Justify your answer.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No. For $0,10$, the median is $\\frac{0+10}{2}=5$, which is not a data value.",
          "worked": "With an even number of values, the median is the mean of the two middle values. For the data $0,10$:\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Median}&=\\frac{0+10}{2}\\\\\\text{Median}&=5\\end{aligned}$$\nThe value $5$ is not in the dataset, so the median need not be a data value. With an odd number of values, the median is the actual middle data value.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      }
    ]
  },
